\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The scalar counting factor of one sector}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Fix a \vocabk{sector labeling}{fdip-0121} #{\lambda} and the
reduced links of \ref{fdip-0123}. Suppose the face, edge and site
factors satisfy the invariance hypotheses of the
\vocabk{extended orbit weight}{fdip-0121}, and
write #{\check\Xi(V)} for the weight of a reduced connection at
the reference scalar of #{\lambda}. With unnormalized counting over
#{k},
##{\check Z_\lambda=\sum_{V\in G^E}\check\Xi(V),\qquad
 Z_\lambda=\Bigl(\prod_{x\in X}|\mathcal O_{\lambda(x)}|\Bigr)
 \check Z_\lambda.}
The product of cardinalities is the corresponding sum of copies of
the unit of #{k}, with no division. The sum of #{\Xi_c} over
#{G^E\times S^X} is the sum of #{Z_\lambda} over all labelings. When
every stabilizer #{G^\circ_{\lambda(x)}} is trivial, the residual
group is trivial, each joint orbit contains exactly
#{\prod_x|\mathcal O_{\lambda(x)}|} configurations, and the scalars
in that orbit are all of the sector. The factor
#{|\mathcal O|^{|X|}} of \ref{fdip-010Y} is the case of one orbit,
and a free orbit makes that factor #{|G|^{|X|}}. The rank of the
invariant functions is the number of residual orbits. The count of gauge orbits of connections alone is
the invariant-tensor count of \ref{fdip-00A9}.}

\proof{
\p{The change of variables in \ref{fdip-0123} is bijective onto the
scalar tuples of the sector times #{G^E}. The gauge with value
#{g_x^{-1}} preserves the weight, by the same cancellation as
\ref{fdip-010C}: faces change by conjugation, edges by the diagonal
invariance, and sites by the invariance of #{c_x}. Every scalar
tuple of the sector therefore contributes the same sum
#{\check Z_\lambda}. Adding those copies gives the product formula.}

\p{A configuration valued in #{S} has a labeling by the orbits of
its values, so the sum over #{G^E\times S^X} splits as the sum of
the sector sums. Each sector sum is the corresponding #{Z_\lambda}.
If every relevant stabilizer is trivial, the action of
#{G} on each such orbit is free and transitive, and
#{|\mathcal O_i|=|G|}. The stabilizer of a reference configuration
is trivial, so its joint orbit has #{|G|^{|X|}} elements. The
scalar component #{(a_x\phi^\circ_{\lambda(x)})_x} meets every
scalar tuple of the sector exactly once as #{a} runs through
#{G^X}. Thus the orbit contains exactly that many scalar
configurations. The indicators of the residual orbits form a basis
of the invariant functions, so the rank equals the number of those
orbits. In the free case the residual group is trivial and the
rank is #{|G|^{|E|}}. \ref{fdip-00A9} counts orbits of #{G^X} on
#{G^E} alone.}
}
